1
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:04,891
Right now, natural gas is moving beneath your feet.

2
00:00:04,891 --> 00:00:09,125
Across mountains, prairie and shield, through steel you will never see.

3
00:00:09,625 --> 00:00:14,361
This is twenty years of that movement, drawn from public records, on a single map.

4
00:00:18,811 --> 00:00:19,915
This is Gas Day.

5
00:00:19,915 --> 00:00:23,989
Every line follows a route on TC Energy's Canadian network,

6
00:00:23,989 --> 00:00:29,306
and every number comes from flow data filed with the Canada Energy Regulator.

7
00:00:29,757 --> 00:00:31,494
Thick lines carry more gas.

8
00:00:31,494 --> 00:00:34,454
Brighter blue means a route is closer to full.

9
00:00:34,454 --> 00:00:37,608
And the pulses show which way the gas is flowing.

10
00:00:39,708 --> 00:00:42,639
Press play, and twenty years run past in seconds.

11
00:00:42,639 --> 00:00:44,913
More than seven thousand days of flow.

12
00:00:45,513 --> 00:00:46,595
Watch the west.

13
00:00:46,595 --> 00:00:51,575
In 2010, about five billion cubic feet a day came down this corridor,

14
00:00:51,575 --> 00:00:54,174
out of Alberta and British Columbia.

15
00:00:54,574 --> 00:00:57,390
By 2025, it was more than twelve.

16
00:01:00,490 --> 00:01:03,312
Click any point, and the panel tells its story.

17
00:01:03,312 --> 00:01:06,015
This is Niagara, on the border with New York.

18
00:01:06,515 --> 00:01:09,711
In 2007, gas flowed east here, out of Canada,

19
00:01:09,711 --> 00:01:13,192
at around eight hundred million cubic feet a day.

20
00:01:13,692 --> 00:01:16,711
Then American shale changed everything.

21
00:01:16,711 --> 00:01:18,027
Watch the pulses.

22
00:01:18,027 --> 00:01:23,292
By 2025, the same pipe was carrying gas the other way, into Ontario.

23
00:01:23,892 --> 00:01:27,433
One chart shows the whole border shifting, region by region.

24
00:01:30,733 --> 00:01:31,679
January 2024.

25
00:01:31,679 --> 00:01:34,738
An Arctic front settles over the prairies,

26
00:01:34,738 --> 00:01:39,181
and Calgary's average temperature falls to minus thirty-four.

27
00:01:39,631 --> 00:01:43,163
Gas delivered toward the city jumps by more than half in a week,

28
00:01:43,163 --> 00:01:45,924
to the highest level this point has ever recorded.

29
00:01:46,524 --> 00:01:48,124
Here, every dot is one day.

30
00:01:48,124 --> 00:01:52,924
The colder it gets, the further right it sits, and the more gas this point moves.

31
00:01:53,524 --> 00:01:57,833
And one grid shows, month by month, which points are running close to full.

32
00:02:00,633 --> 00:02:05,653
Now switch on the wildfire layer, and go back to the summer of 2023.

33
00:02:05,653 --> 00:02:09,124
The worst fire season Canada has ever recorded.

34
00:02:09,624 --> 00:02:11,682
Each orange shape is a large fire.

35
00:02:11,682 --> 00:02:13,074
By this map's estimate,

36
00:02:13,074 --> 00:02:18,584
more than half a million hectares burned within fifty kilometres of these routes that year.

37
00:02:19,084 --> 00:02:20,611
And the red dots are live:

38
00:02:20,611 --> 00:02:23,607
satellite hotspots from the last twenty-four hours.

39
00:02:26,557 --> 00:02:27,763
No private feeds.

40
00:02:27,763 --> 00:02:28,615
No licences.

41
00:02:28,615 --> 00:02:34,365
Only public data, a time slider, and a new way to see a network that never stops.

42
00:02:35,265 --> 00:02:36,417
This is Gas Day.
